@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Operators";
}

<div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">

    <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

        <!-- PAGE HEADER -->

        <div class="mb-4">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Operators in Java
            </h2>

            <p class="lead text-muted mb-0">
                Learn how Java operators are used to perform arithmetic,
                comparison, logical, assignment, bitwise, and conditional
                operations.
            </p>

        </div>


        <!-- WHAT ARE OPERATORS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What is an Operator?
            </h3>

            <p>
                An operator is a symbol that tells Java to perform an
                operation on one or more values or variables.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int a = 10;

int b = 5;

int result = a + b;</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Here, <code>+</code> is the arithmetic operator.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- OPERATOR TYPES -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-4">
                Types of Operators
            </h3>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Operator Type</th>
                            <th>Examples</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Arithmetic</td>
                            <td><code>+ - * / %</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Unary</td>
                            <td><code>++ -- + - ! ~</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Relational</td>
                            <td><code>== != &gt; &lt; &gt;= &lt;=</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Logical</td>
                            <td><code>&amp;&amp; || !</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Assignment</td>
                            <td><code>= += -= *= /= %=</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Bitwise</td>
                            <td><code>&amp; | ^ ~</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Shift</td>
                            <td><code>&lt;&lt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Conditional</td>
                            <td><code>? :</code></td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- ARITHMETIC -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                1. Arithmetic Operators
            </h3>

            <p>
                Arithmetic operators are used to perform mathematical
                calculations.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Operator</th>
                            <th>Meaning</th>
                            <th>Example</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>+</code></td>
                            <td>Addition</td>
                            <td><code>a + b</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>-</code></td>
                            <td>Subtraction</td>
                            <td><code>a - b</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>*</code></td>
                            <td>Multiplication</td>
                            <td><code>a * b</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>/</code></td>
                            <td>Division</td>
                            <td><code>a / b</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>%</code></td>
                            <td>Remainder</td>
                            <td><code>a % b</code></td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>


            <div class="code-block mt-4">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int a = 20;

int b = 6;

System.out.println(a + b);

System.out.println(a - b);

System.out.println(a * b);

System.out.println(a / b);

System.out.println(a % b);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
                Output:
            </p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>26
14
120
3
2</code></pre>

</div>


        </section>


        <!-- STRING CONCATENATION -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Addition with Strings
            </h3>

            <p>
                The <code>+</code> operator also performs string concatenation
                when one operand is a String.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String name = "CIIT Institute 🤓 ";

int startingYear = 2014;

System.out.println(name + " Established in " + startingYear);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Output:
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Output
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>CIIT Institute 🤓 Established in 2014
</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- UNARY -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                2. Unary Operators
            </h3>

            <p>
                Unary operators operate on a single operand.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Operator</th>
                            <th>Meaning</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>+</code></td>
                            <td>Positive value</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>-</code></td>
                            <td>Negates a numeric value</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>++</code></td>
                            <td>Increment by one</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>--</code></td>
                            <td>Decrement by one</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>!</code></td>
                            <td>Logical negation</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>~</code></td>
                            <td>Bitwise complement</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>


            <div class="code-block mt-4">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int count = 10;

count++;

System.out.println(count);

count--;

System.out.println(count);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
                Output:
            </p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>11
10</code></pre>

</div>

        </section>


        <!-- PRE INCREMENT -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Pre-Increment and Post-Increment
            </h3>

            <p>
                The position of <code>++</code> determines whether the value
                is incremented before or after its current value is used.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int a = 5;

int x = ++a;

System.out.println(a);

System.out.println(x);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>6
6</code></pre>

</div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                In pre-increment, the variable is incremented before the
                value is assigned to <code>x</code>.
            </p>


            <div class="code-block mt-4">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int b = 5;

int y = b++;

System.out.println(b);

System.out.println(y);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>6
5</code></pre>

</div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                In post-increment, the current value is used first and then
                the variable is incremented.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- RELATIONAL -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                3. Relational Operators
            </h3>

            <p>
                Relational operators compare two values and produce a
                boolean result.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Operator</th>
                            <th>Meaning</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>==</code></td>
                            <td>Equal to</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>!=</code></td>
                            <td>Not equal to</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Greater than</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&lt;</code></td>
                            <td>Less than</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&gt;=</code></td>
                            <td>Greater than or equal to</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&lt;=</code></td>
                            <td>Less than or equal to</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>


            <div class="code-block mt-4">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int age = 25;

System.out.println(age &gt;= 18);

System.out.println(age == 25);

System.out.println(age &lt; 18);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>true
true
false</code></pre>

</div>

        </section>


        <!-- EQUALITY -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                == with Objects and Strings
            </h3>

            <p>
                For primitive values, <code>==</code> compares values.
                For object references, <code>==</code> checks whether two
                references refer to the same object.
            </p>

            <p>
                To compare String contents, use <code>equals()</code>.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String a = new String("CIIT");

String b = new String("CIIT");

System.out.println(a == b);

System.out.println(a.equals(b));</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="alert alert-info mt-3">

                <strong>Remember:</strong>
                <span>
                    Use <code>equals()</code> when comparing the contents of
                    String objects.
                </span>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- LOGICAL -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                4. Logical Operators
            </h3>

            <p>
                Logical operators combine boolean expressions.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Operator</th>
                            <th>Meaning</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&amp;&amp;</code></td>
                            <td>Logical AND</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>||</code></td>
                            <td>Logical OR</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>!</code></td>
                            <td>Logical NOT</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>


            <div class="code-block mt-4">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int age = 25;

boolean hasId = true;

boolean eligible = age &gt;= 18 &amp;&amp; hasId;

System.out.println(eligible);</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- SHORT CIRCUIT -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Short-Circuit Evaluation
            </h3>

            <p>
                Java's <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> operators
                use short-circuit evaluation.
            </p>

            <p>
                With <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, if the first condition is false,
                the remaining conditions may not be evaluated. With
                <code>||</code>, if the first condition is true, the remaining
                conditions may not be evaluated.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int age = 15;

if (age &gt;= 18 &amp;&amp; age &lt;= 60) {

    System.out.println("Eligible");

}</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- ASSIGNMENT -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                5. Assignment Operators
            </h3>

            <p>
                Assignment operators assign values to variables.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Operator</th>
                            <th>Equivalent</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>=</code></td>
                            <td><code>a = b</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>+=</code></td>
                            <td><code>a = a + b</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>-=</code></td>
                            <td><code>a = a - b</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>*=</code></td>
                            <td><code>a = a * b</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>/=</code></td>
                            <td><code>a = a / b</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>%=</code></td>
                            <td><code>a = a % b</code></td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>


            <div class="code-block mt-4">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int value = 10;

value += 5;

value *= 2;

System.out.println(value);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>30</code></pre>

</div>

        </section>


        <!-- BITWISE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                6. Bitwise Operators
            </h3>

            <p>
                Bitwise operators operate on the individual bits of integral
                values.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Operator</th>
                            <th>Meaning</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&amp;</code></td>
                            <td>Bitwise AND</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>|</code></td>
                            <td>Bitwise OR</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>^</code></td>
                            <td>Bitwise XOR</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>~</code></td>
                            <td>Bitwise complement</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>


            <div class="code-block mt-4">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int a = 5;

int b = 3;

System.out.println(a &amp; b);

System.out.println(a | b);

System.out.println(a ^ b);

System.out.println(~a);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
                Output:
            </p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>1
7
6
-6</code></pre>

</div>
        </section>


        <!-- SHIFT -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                7. Shift Operators
            </h3>

            <p>
                Shift operators move the bits of an integral value.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Operator</th>
                            <th>Meaning</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&lt;&lt;</code></td>
                            <td>Left shift</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&gt;&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Signed right shift</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Unsigned right shift</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>


            <div class="code-block mt-4">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int number = 8;

System.out.println(number &lt;&lt; 1);

System.out.println(number &gt;&gt; 1);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
                Output:
            </p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>16
4</code></pre>

</div>

        </section>


        <!-- TERNARY -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                8. Ternary Operator
            </h3>

            <p>
                The ternary operator is a compact conditional expression with
                three operands.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int age = 20;

String result = age &gt;= 18 ? "Adult" : "Minor";

System.out.println(result);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
                Output:
            </p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Adult
</code></pre>

</div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Syntax:
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Syntax
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse</code></pre>

            </div>

            

        </section>


        <!-- OPERATOR PRECEDENCE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Operator Precedence
            </h3>

            <p>
                When an expression contains multiple operators, Java follows
                defined precedence and associativity rules.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int result = 10 + 5 * 2;

System.out.println(result);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Multiplication has higher precedence than addition, so the
                multiplication is evaluated first.
            </p>

            <p>
                Use parentheses when you want the evaluation order to be
                explicit.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int result = (10 + 5) * 2;

System.out.println(result);</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- COMPLETE EXAMPLE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Complete Example
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">
                    Java
                </div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>public class Operators {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int marks = 85;

        int bonus = 5;

        int total = marks + bonus;

        boolean passed = total &gt;= 40;

        String result = passed ? "Pass" : "Fail";

        System.out.println("Total: " + total);

        System.out.println("Result: " + result);

    }

}</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
                Output:
            </p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Total: 90
Result: Pass
</code></pre>

</div>

        </section>




        <!-- BEST PRACTICES -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Operator Best Practices
            </h3>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use parentheses when an expression could be difficult
                    to understand.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Avoid unnecessarily complex expressions.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Remember that integer division discards the fractional
                    part.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use <code>equals()</code> for String content comparison.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use short-circuit operators when appropriate for boolean
                    conditions.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Be careful with overflow when performing arithmetic on
                    fixed-width integer types.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </section>


        <!-- SUMMARY -->

        <section class="mb-4">

            <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

                <h5 class="fw-bold">
                    Summary
                </h5>

                <p class="mb-0">
                    Java provides a rich set of operators for arithmetic,
                    comparison, logical operations, assignment, bitwise
                    manipulation, shifting, and conditional expressions.
                    Understanding operator behavior and precedence is
                    essential for writing correct Java expressions.
                </p>

            </div>

        </section>


